# Backend: notification service

Service at `backend/services/notification/`, app package `notification/notification/`. **No direct HTTP API today.** Entry points are message handlers consuming RabbitMQ (via the messaging abstraction).

## Flow

```text
Auth/other services → RabbitMQ → handlers → services → Jinja templates → email/channel
```

## Package map

```text
notification/
├── core/          # config / settings
├── handlers/      # queue consumers / message entry points; handler_routes.py maps queue → handler
├── services/      # business helpers: template_service.py (Jinja), email_service.py (SMTP)
├── messaging/     # base abstraction + rabbitmq/
├── channels/      # delivery channels (placeholder today)
├── exceptions/    # definitions (+ handlers for retry / dead letter)
├── schemas/       # message/payload schemas
├── templates/     # Jinja templates for notification content (extend base.html)
└── types/         # shared types / enums
```

## Conventions

- Add new message flows as handlers that delegate to `services/`; keep queue wiring out of business logic.
- Render content with Jinja under `templates/`; do not hard-code large email bodies in Python.
- Use the messaging abstraction; do not scatter RabbitMQ-specific calls in handlers or services.
- Keep exception definitions centralized under `exceptions/`.
- Every queue has a dead-letter queue and a `*_EMAIL_RETRY_COUNT`; handlers validate the message schema and are idempotent.

Details: `docs/backend/notification.md`, `docs/guides/email-templates.md`, `docs/guides/messaging.md`.
